# RESTful API Server (to be rebuilt)

The previous API server binary has been removed. The new RESTful API server
will be written from scratch in one of the following languages:

  1. C++   — smallest runtime footprint, no toolchain on target needed once
             compiled to a static binary; more manual memory/HTTP handling.
  2. Rust  — memory-safe by design, strong HTTP/async ecosystem (axum, actix),
             single static binary; recommended for a security-sensitive,
             network-exposed service.

## Required behavior (matches docs/API.md)

- Bearer-token authentication (tokens stored in /etc/api/key, one per line).
- Listen on 127.0.0.1:9000 only (Nginx/HAProxy terminate TLS and proxy /api).
- Dispatch JSON requests to the command handlers in /usr/local/sbin/api/*.
- Enforce: rate limiting (10 req/min/IP), input validation, request size limit.
- Never run handlers with shell string interpolation of untrusted input.
- Run under the hardened systemd unit (server.service) with least privilege.

## Security requirements for the rebuild

- Validate/whitelist every field before passing to handlers (defense in depth;
  handlers already validate, but the server must too).
- Constant-time token comparison; do not log tokens.
- Reject oversized bodies; timeouts on handler execution.
- Bind strictly to localhost; rely on Nginx for TLS and exposure.

Until the new server is built, `server.service` is installed but NOT started.
